C = [
("h1", "Preface"),
("p", "This volume argues a position. It does not survey the ways Adam and Christ have been read and leave the choice to the reader. It says what the author believes Paul teaches when he sets the two men side by side, it gives the evidence from Paul’s own sentences, and it answers the strongest objections in the objectors’ own words."),
("p", "The position is this. Paul sets Adam and Christ side by side twice, once in Romans 5:12–21 and once in 1 Corinthians 15:21–22 and 45–49, and the two passages do not answer the same question. Romans 5 asks how sin, death and righteousness reached “all men.” First Corinthians 15 asks how the dead are raised, and with what body they come. “In Adam” and “in Christ” name two headships, and the two headships do not have the same membership: the first reaches every descendant of the first man, and the second reaches those who are in the last Adam, who “receive abundance of grace and of the gift of righteousness” (Romans 5:17). The two passages are therefore not one doctrine said twice."),
("p", "Three things about the method. First, the volume reads the King James Version only, and every Scripture quotation is checked word for word against it. Second, the volume reads each passage in its place: the chapters before and after, and the argument that Paul is making in the letter. A sentence about Adam in the middle of a letter about justification is not the same as a sentence about Adam in the middle of a chapter about the resurrection, and the difference is the subject of this book. Third, where modern writers are named, they are named for what they say. A writer is quoted only from a text the author has read in full and that anyone can read for free. Otherwise the writer’s position is described in the author’s words, and no view is credited to anyone who has not been read."),
("p", "The volume is written for readers who know the two passages and want to see them together. It does not require Greek. A few Greek words that carry weight are given in Roman letters in the appendix on key words, with the way the King James Version renders them. The reader is asked to keep a Bible open, to test every row of every table, and to correct the author where the text does."),
("p", "The author does not claim to have said the last word on two of the most discussed passages in the New Testament. The claim is narrower. When Romans 5 and 1 Corinthians 15 are each read as answering the question Paul put to them, a number of old difficulties become smaller, and a number of old arguments lose the weight they were asked to carry."),
("h1", "Thesis"),
("p", "Paul sets Adam and Christ side by side twice, and the two passages do not answer the same question."),
("ctbl", "The thesis in four statements.", [
    ("Statement", "What it says", "Where it is argued"),
    ("1", "Romans 5:12–21 asks how sin, death and righteousness came to “all men,” and it answers with one man’s trespass and one man’s obedience.", "Chapters 3 to 7, and 13"),
    ("2", "The scope of “all” and “many” in Romans 5 is read clause by clause inside Paul’s own sentences, and “they which receive” (5:17) is part of the sentence.", "Chapters 5 and 6"),
    ("3", "First Corinthians 15:21–22 and 45–49 ask how the dead are raised and with what body: the first man and the last Adam, the natural and then the spiritual, the earthy and the heavenly image.", "Chapters 8 to 13"),
    ("4", "“In Adam” and “in Christ” name two headships with different membership. The two passages are not one doctrine said twice, and neither enrols Israel’s covenants in either head.", "Chapters 14 and 15"),
  ], [0.15, 0.57, 0.28]),
("p", "A reader may test the thesis in one move. Take Romans 5:12–21 and ask, of every clause, what question it is answering. Then take 1 Corinthians 15:20–28 and 15:35–50 and ask the same. If the clauses answer the same question, the thesis fails. If they answer different questions, the thesis stands, and the reader will have found out why the same two names appear in both."),
]
